<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
import Contact from './lib/Contact.abide'
import Advanced from './lib/Advanced.abide'
import BillingContact from './lib/BillingContact.abide'
import Assign from './lib/Assign.abide'
import contactSource from './lib/Contact.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import advancedSource from './lib/Advanced.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import billingContactSource from './lib/BillingContact.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import assignSource from './lib/Assign.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A text input binds with `bind:value`">
        <Example
            id="bind-text"
            title="`bind:value` — the field and the value are one copy"
            summary="A contact's email and plan, with the line underneath showing what a save would send. Type into the field."
            files={{
                'lib/Contact.abide': contactSource,
            }}
        >
            <Contact />
        </Example>

        <p><em>2 lines, 1 direction each</em> — and no handler between them.</p>

        <p><code>bind:value</code> reads the property and writes back on <code>input</code> or
        <code>change</code>. The value is the single copy — there is no separate form model to keep
        in step with it, and no submit handler needed to find out what the fields hold. A
        <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> take the same binding.</p>

        <p>The hand-written arm is a listener <strong>and</strong> a seeding line per field, and the
        seeding half is the one that gets forgotten: the page looks right until something other than
        the keyboard writes the value.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A checkbox and a `<details>` bind to a boolean">
        <Example
            id="bind-boolean"
            title="`bind:checked` and `bind:open` — a boolean, not a string"
            summary="A reminders checkbox and a disclosure. Tick the box and open the panel, and watch the two lines underneath."
            files={{
                'lib/Advanced.abide': advancedSource,
            }}
        >
            <Advanced />
        </Example>

        <p><code>bind:checked</code> is the boolean form. It mirrors a boolean DOM property as a
        boolean attribute and <strong>never stringifies it</strong>, so a <code>false</code> is an
        absent attribute rather than the string <code>"false"</code> — which makes
        <code>{'checked={false}'}</code> behave. It writes back on <code>change</code>.</p>

        <p><code>bind:open</code> is the same binding on a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, written
        back from its <code>toggle</code> event, so a disclosure's open state is somewhere you can
        read rather than somewhere in the DOM.</p>

        <Snippet source={advancedSource} path="Advanced.abide" from={['<details bind:open', '<li><span>advanced']} />

        <p>The readout reads <code>showAdvanced</code> like any other value, which is the point of
        binding it: the disclosure is a value, so the rest of the page can branch on it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A bound value decides what renders">
        <Example
            id="bind-branch"
            title={'`{#if}` over a bound value — the field decides what renders'}
            summary="A contact form that only asks for a billing address when you say there is one. Tick the box."
            files={{
                'lib/BillingContact.abide': billingContactSource,
            }}
        >
            <BillingContact />
        </Example>

        <p><code>{'{#if billSeparately}'}</code> reads the bound value like any other read, so the
        field mounts when the box is ticked and unmounts when it is cleared. There is no listener
        calling a second render and no teardown for the field it built — the hand-written arm
        carries both.</p>

        <p>What was typed into the address goes with it, the input being unbound. Bind it too where
        it has to survive the box being cleared and ticked again.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A radio group binds to one value, a checkbox set to an array">
        <Example
            id="bind-group"
            title="`bind:group` — one value for radios, an array for checkboxes"
            summary="A contact's tier and the regions they cover. Change the tier, then tick and untick a region."
            files={{
                'lib/Assign.abide': assignSource,
            }}
        >
            <Assign />
        </Example>

        <p><code>bind:group</code> is membership rather than a value. Each input is compared against
        <strong>its own <code>value</code></strong>, and <code>group</code> is never emitted as an
        attribute — it is a binding, not something that reaches the DOM.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<label>
    <input type="radio" bind:group={plan} value="monthly"> Monthly
</label>
<label>
    <input type="radio" bind:group={plan} value="yearly"> Yearly
</label>`} />

        <p>Point several checkboxes at one array and the same binding collects them, each
        contributing its own <code>value</code>.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<label>
    <input type="checkbox" bind:group={topics} value="releases"> Releases
</label>
<label>
    <input type="checkbox" bind:group={topics} value="security"> Security
</label>`} />

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/show-markup-conditionally')}>Conditionals</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/repeat-markup-over-a-list')}>Lists</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`bind:` takes any writable `Reactive`">
        <p><code>bind:</code> needs somewhere to write. A value's name and a member path both
        qualify; an expression does not, because <code>{'bind:value={price * 2}'}</code> has nothing
        to write back to.</p>

        <p>Where the value you want is computed, hand over the pair explicitly:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<input bind:value={{ get: () => cents / 100, set: (v) => cents = v * 100 }}>`} />

        <p>The accessor pair is the general form. Naming a value is the sugar over it.</p>

        <p>A derived value is writable too, and where its body hands back another
        <code>Reactive</code> the write goes through to that one (11.59). So <code>bind:value</code>
        over <code>{'reactive(() => users({ id: selected }))'}</code> edits the shared
        <code>{'users({ id })'}</code> entry, and every other reader of that entry sees the
        edit.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `schema` refuses a value as it is written">
        <p>A <code>schema</code> runs on the way into a value, and a write it refuses is rejected at
        the boundary rather than stored and checked later:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/account/index.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { reactive } from '@abide/abide'
import { z } from 'zod'

const email = reactive<string>(undefined, { schema: z.string().email() })
</script>

<input bind:value={email}>
{#if email.error()}
    <p class="error">{email.error().data[0]}</p>
{/if}`} />

        <p><code>error()</code> hands back the failure <strong>without throwing</strong>, so a
        rejected field renders a message rather than reaching <code>{'{#try}'}</code>. A schema
        refusal is a <code>ValidationError</code>, and on a primitive its <code>data</code> is the
        list of messages.</p>

        <p>The field opens empty. An opening <code>undefined</code> lands without passing the schema
        (4.16), so an unfilled field is not a refusal.</p>

        <p><strong>The field is not clobbered while you type.</strong> A refused write does not move
        the stored value, so nothing wakes the read-back side of the binding and the <code>f</code>
        you just typed stays where it is. <code>error()</code> is a separate signal, so the message
        renders beside it. Two rules you already have, doing the work between them.</p>

        <p><code>set</code> returns the refusal too, so a submit handler can act on it at the call
        site instead of reading back.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `transform` refuses under a name you declared">
        <p>Both gates refuse. What you refuse <strong>with</strong> picks between them: a schema
        refuses as <code>ValidationError</code>, and a <code>transform</code> refuses as a name of
        your own — the only way a failure arrives carrying data you chose. <code>refuse.named</code>
        declares it once and resolves on both sides, so the same name serves a form field and a
        handler.</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/lib/failures.ts" code={`import { refuse } from '@abide/abide'
import { z } from 'zod'

export const notAnEmail = refuse.named('NotAnEmail', 422, undefined, {
    schema: z.object({ value: z.string() }),
})`} />

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/account/index.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { reactive } from '@abide/abide'
import { notAnEmail } from '#src/lib/failures'

const email = reactive(undefined, {
    transform: (value: string) =>
        value.includes('@') ? value : notAnEmail({ value }),
})
</script>

<input bind:value={email}>
{#if email.isError(email.error(), 'NotAnEmail')}
    <p class="error">{email.error().data.value} is not an email address.</p>
{/if}`} />

        <p>No type argument is spelled: the refusal is typed onto <code>error()</code> from what the
        transform hands back, and annotating its parameter says what the field takes.</p>

        <p><code>isError</code> narrows it by name, and <code>.data</code> is typed to what that
        failure declared — the same two calls a page makes on a refused request. That is the reason
        to reach for it over a schema here: <code>.data.value</code> is the rejected text, which a
        <code>ValidationError</code> does not carry.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component prop writes back to the caller with `bind:`">
        <p>The call site decides this, not the child.</p>

        <p>A prop is a <strong>live read</strong> of the caller's expression.
        <code>{'count={total}'}</code> tracks <code>total</code>, <code>{'count={total * 2}'}</code>
        tracks it as a derived read, and neither reinstantiates the component. The child declares
        the prop as the type of its value and writes it by name:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/lib/Stepper.abide" code={`<script>
import { props } from '@abide/abide'

const { count } = props<{ count: number }>()
</script>

<button onclick={() => count += 1}>{count}</button>`} />

        <p>Put <code>bind:</code> on the tag and the write goes to the caller's value:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<Stepper bind:count={total}/>`} />

        <p>Leave <code>bind:</code> off and the write stays in the child until the caller's
        expression changes. The same <code>Stepper</code> then works bound and unbound, and a reader
        sees which one it is at the call site without opening the child.</p>

        <p>On a component, <code>bind:</code> takes the name of a reactive value, or a
        <code>{'{get, set}'}</code> pair as an input does. <code>{'bind:count={total * 2}'}</code>
        has nothing to write to, and the checker says so.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates/respond-to-a-click')}>Events</a> — when you actually want a
            handler</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a> — the thing being
            bound</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a> — checking a body
            at the boundary</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
